//! The hostile-container corpus and the harness a reader has to satisfy (issue P2.7).
//!
//! An untrusted `.pvec` is refused, not trusted. This module writes the attacks first, so the safe
//! reader (P4.5), the integrity work (P4.6), the capability negotiation (P4.7) and the resource limits
//! (P4.8) are built against them, and the fuzzing of P7.8 and the browser fixtures of M2.7 start from
//! the same mutation families.
//!
//! Four pieces:
//!
//! * [`bases`]: valid artifacts, generated by the P2.4 generator (and, for the two shapes no corpus
//!   record maps onto yet, by the writer directly), that every case is a mutation of;
//! * [`mutate`]: a mutation is a short list of byte-level [`Op`]s over a base, never a hand-built file,
//!   so repairing it is putting the base back;
//! * [`catalog`]: the cases, one named case per attack, each with the rules a refusal may name;
//! * [`harness`]: [`harness::run`] holds any [`harness::Reader`] to every case it claims to provide:
//!   a typed refusal naming the rule, no panic, no hang, a bounded allocation, and the repaired file
//!   accepted.
//!
//! The reference reader in `crate::reference` satisfies every case that needs only the parsers that
//! exist. A case that needs something a later issue builds says so in [`catalog::Requires`], and is
//! counted as skipped until a reader provides it.

pub mod bases;
pub mod catalog;
pub mod harness;
pub mod mutate;

pub use bases::{Base, bases};
pub use catalog::{Case, Expect, Family, Requires, catalog};
pub use harness::{Failure, Limits, Meter, NoMeter, Reader, ReferenceReader, Refusal, Report, Verdict, run};
pub use mutate::{Layout, Op, apply};
